WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:09.137
Proxima Centauri is the nearest star to the Sun, about 4.2 light-years away.

2
00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:13.646
A planet, Proxima b, goes around it once in about 11 days.

3
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.168
Some scientists study small probes that could fly past this planet.

4
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.204
At one fifth of the speed of light, the trip takes more than 20 years.

5
00:00:22.687 --> 00:00:26.072
Aiming such a probe needs a prediction, made before launch,

6
00:00:26.072 --> 00:00:28.998
of where the planet will be when the probe arrives.

7
00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:34.386
This paper asks a simple question: how well can we know that position before launch?

8
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.706
Picture a flat sheet at the planet, at right angles to the probe's path.

9
00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:41.608
We call it the target plane.

10
00:00:42.053 --> 00:00:45.972
The data have errors, so the paper cannot predict one exact point.

11
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.858
Instead, it gets a cloud of possible positions on the sheet.

12
00:00:50.286 --> 00:00:54.144
Now draw the smallest circle that holds 90% of the cloud.

13
00:00:54.586 --> 00:00:56.683
Its radius is called r₉₀ ("r ninety").

14
00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.194
A small r₉₀ means a sharp prediction. A large r₉₀ means a vague one.

15
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.650
How small is small enough?

16
00:01:05.086 --> 00:01:11.006
One published study says the total error must be about 100,000 km or less.

17
00:01:11.453 --> 00:01:14.951
That is about one quarter of the distance from the Earth to the Moon.

18
00:01:15.386 --> 00:01:20.321
The paper uses this value as one mark, not as the only possible requirement.

19
00:01:20.753 --> 00:01:26.065
So it tests 13 radii, from 10,000 km to 10 million km.

20
00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:30.591
To make the prediction, the paper combines two kinds of data.

21
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.339
The Gaia space telescope measured the star's position,

22
00:01:34.339 --> 00:01:36.552
distance, and motion across the sky.

23
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.671
Radial velocities measure how fast the star moves toward us and away from us,

24
00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:43.242
as the planet pulls on it.

25
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.160
The model moves the star along its path,

26
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.942
with a small pull from the bright pair Alpha Centauri A and B.

27
00:01:50.386 --> 00:01:53.245
It moves the planet along its orbit around the star.

28
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.823
Another planet, Proxima d, and Einstein's gravity slowly turn the orbit of b.

29
00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:02.268
If the model leaves out planet d,

30
00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:07.056
the predicted position moves by 36,000 to 212,000 km.

31
00:02:07.486 --> 00:02:10.332
That is about the same size as the mark. The

32
00:02:10.332 --> 00:02:13.629
model for future observations includes this effect.

33
00:02:14.652 --> 00:02:15.890
Next, a fair test.

34
00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:20.558
The paper fixed its rules in writing before it ran each computation.

35
00:02:20.986 --> 00:02:23.415
It also uses eight versions of the model.

36
00:02:23.852 --> 00:02:26.459
They make different choices about the planet,

37
00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:28.949
the star, and the orientation of the orbit.

38
00:02:29.386 --> 00:02:34.980
A test radius counts as reached only if r₉₀ is inside it in all eight versions.

39
00:02:35.419 --> 00:02:38.665
If r₉₀ is outside it in all eight, it is not reached.

40
00:02:39.086 --> 00:02:42.283
If the versions disagree, the answer is undecided.

41
00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:44.676
And undecided never means no.

42
00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:47.582
So what do today's data give?

43
00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:54.766
At the launch years on the list, r₉₀ is 7.2 to 9.45 million km.

44
00:02:55.186 --> 00:03:00.441
That is about the size of the planet's orbit, and about 70 to 95 times the mark.

45
00:03:00.886 --> 00:03:04.766
Better data for any single input, alone, does not reach the mark.

46
00:03:05.186 --> 00:03:09.527
Among the largest parts of the error is the orientation of the orbit:

47
00:03:09.527 --> 00:03:11.603
how the orbit is turned in space.

48
00:03:12.052 --> 00:03:14.094
Radial velocities do not measure it.

49
00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:18.600
Next, the paper adds possible future observations.

50
00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:21.670
Their quality is an assumption, not a forecast.

51
00:03:22.119 --> 00:03:26.414
The list includes pictures that show the planet apart from its star,

52
00:03:26.414 --> 00:03:27.867
more precise Gaia data,

53
00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:32.351
more radial velocities, and a more precise speed of the star toward us.

54
00:03:32.786 --> 00:03:37.545
There is one more problem. The planet goes around its star in about 11 days,

55
00:03:37.545 --> 00:03:40.363
so a change of a few days in the arrival time

56
00:03:40.363 --> 00:03:43.808
puts the planet at a very different place on its orbit.

57
00:03:44.252 --> 00:03:47.763
So the paper checks 24 launch times spread over one orbit,

58
00:03:47.763 --> 00:03:50.062
and the rule must hold at all of them.

59
00:03:50.486 --> 00:03:51.829
With this check,

60
00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:55.944
the smallest test radius with a definite earliest

61
00:03:55.944 --> 00:04:01.067
launch year is 316,000 km at one fifth of the speed of light,

62
00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:03.419
and 562,000 km at one tenth.

63
00:04:03.852 --> 00:04:09.573
At 100,000 km, no setting gives a definite earliest launch year over the orbit.

64
00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:17.714
In one example, the largest r₉₀ of the eight versions moves between about 83,000 and

65
00:04:17.714 --> 00:04:21.195
144,000 km as the launch time changes.

66
00:04:21.619 --> 00:04:24.865
The six settings that the paper checked over the orbit

67
00:04:24.865 --> 00:04:28.411
combine those pictures with much more precise Gaia data and

68
00:04:28.411 --> 00:04:29.132
star speeds.

69
00:04:29.552 --> 00:04:32.779
If you check only one launch time in each year,

70
00:04:32.779 --> 00:04:37.858
these six settings do reach 100,000 km at one fifth of the speed of light.

71
00:04:38.286 --> 00:04:44.174
Without pictures of the planet, no combination on the list gets below 10 million km.

72
00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:47.455
The paper is careful about its limits.

73
00:04:47.886 --> 00:04:53.649
The r₉₀ values come from the model, and their calibration has not been verified:

74
00:04:53.649 --> 00:04:59.628
no one has yet confirmed that a 90% circle holds the true position 90% of the time.

75
00:05:00.052 --> 00:05:02.928
The future instrument performances are assumptions,

76
00:05:02.928 --> 00:05:06.536
and the paper does not claim that the observations will be made.

77
00:05:06.986 --> 00:05:10.884
It also says nothing about whether or when a mission is possible,

78
00:05:10.884 --> 00:05:12.564
or about life on the planet.

79
00:05:13.786 --> 00:05:18.054
In short, the paper measures how far today's knowledge is from the mark,

80
00:05:18.054 --> 00:05:20.900
and which observations on its list come closest.

81
00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:24.064
The paper and its files are open on Zenodo.

82
00:05:24.486 --> 00:05:28.461
This video was made with the help of AI, and the voice is synthetic.
